"Ode to Billie Joe" takes the form of a [[first-person narrative]] by the young daughter of a [[Mississippi Delta]] family. It offers fragments of mealtime conversation on the day that a local boy, an acquaintance of the narrator, jumped to his death from a nearby bridge. The account is interspersed with everyday, polite, mealtime conversation. The song's last verse conveys the passage of events over the following year.{{sfn|Sullivan, Steve|2013|p=114}}

{{Listen |type=music |filename=Bobbie Gentry Ode To Billie Joe.ogg|title=Excerpt of Bobbie Gentry's "Ode to Billie Joe"|description=As the narrator returns home, her mother tells the family of Billie Joe's suicide.}}

The song begins on June&nbsp;3 with the narrator, her brother, and her father returning from farming chores to the family house for dinner (i.e., the midday meal, called "lunch" in other parts of the United States).<ref>The term "dinner," in the American South where the song is set, refers to the meal served generally around noon and called "lunch" in other parts of the country.  "Supper" is the term for the evening meal, which is called "dinner" elsewhere.</ref> After reminding them to wipe their feet, the mother announces she has received news from Choctaw Ridge: "Billie Joe McAllister jumped off the [[Tallahatchie River|Tallahatchie Bridge]]."{{sfn|Elicker, Martina|1997|p=83}} The verse is repeated through the song as the story develops to "heighten the mystery."{{sfn|Toft, Robert|2010|p=8}} Unmoved, the father comments that "Billie Joe never had a lick of sense" before asking for the biscuits and adding "there's five more acres in the [[lower 40]] I've got to plow."{{sfn|Toft, Robert|p=9|2010}} The brother then expresses his surprise, but continues eating his meal.{{sfn|Toft, Robert|2010|p=10}}

The mother notices her daughter is distraught and not eating.{{sfn|Murtha, Tara|2014|p=X}} She mentions the "young preacher" Brother Taylor visited the house earlier and that they would have dinner with him on Sunday. As an afterthought, the mother adds the preacher saw Billie Joe with a girl that "looked a lot" like the daughter and that "she and Billie Joe was throwin' somethin' off the Tallahatchie Bridge."{{sfn|Toft, Robert|2010|p=10}} A year later, the brother has married and moved to [[Tupelo, Mississippi]], while the father has died of an unnamed virus. Though she expresses no sadness over her father's death, the daughter notices her mother is still distressed by it. Rather than consoling her mother, she routinely picks flowers and throws them off the bridge.{{sfn|Elicker, Martina|1997|p=86}}

The song became a success because it created listener curiosity, given that Gentry did not mention what was thrown off of the bridge nor why Billie Joe committed suicide.{{sfn|Toft, Robert|2010|p=11}} It features [[Perfect and imperfect rhymes|perfect rhymes]] from the first to the sixth line of every verse. Meanwhile, the fifth and sixth lines of the song repeat the rhyme of "ridge" and "bridge" in every stanza. The song does not have a chorus.

