The staff of ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' welcomed the release as "fascinating material and performance" with a "potent lyric content that is worth the unusual length of the disk".{{sfn|Billboard staff|1967|p=16}} The ''Los Angeles Times'' critic [[Leonard Feather]] considered it an "aural parallel" to the film ''[[In the Heat of the Night (film)|In the Heat of the Night]]'' (1967), deeming them both "sardonic, knife-edge studies of human nature". Feather concluded Gentry added "a durable new dimension" to American "contemporary folklore".{{sfn|Feather, Leonard|1967|p=C 13}} ''The New York Times'' commented on the success of the song four weeks after its release. Critic [[John S. Wilson (music critic)|John S. Wilson]] felt the song was "a most unlikely candidate for success," as it was "long by radio programming standards" and he considered the topic "nothing startling, nothing strange, nothing particularly original," Wilson remarked the lyrics had "something to say about indifference ... which, after a couple of clarifying hearings, drifts off into the midst of forgotten poesy".{{sfn|Wilson, John|1967|p=30 D}}

[[Nixon Smiley]] wrote in his piece for the ''[[Miami Herald]]'' that "not since [[William Faulkner]] wrote ''[[As I Lay Dying]]'' has anything come out of Mississippi as earthy and as fundamental" as 'Ode to Billie Joe.'" Smiley determined that upon "casual hearing there seems to be nothing great about the song, the lyrics or the rendition," but that it "captivated both the young and old." He noted disc jockeys were "surprised, even flabbergasted," and "sometimes disgusted".{{sfn|Smiley, Nixon|1967|p=3 G}}

The ''[[Montgomery Advertiser]]'' found the song "hard to classify." It remarked that it "has [a] rhythm and blues beat, and it's clever," and also noted the presence of mystery.{{sfn|Wolfe, Thomas|1967|p=D1}} The ''[[Fort Worth Star-Telegram]]'' called it "one of the most haunting songs of the year."{{sfn|Stewart, Perry|1967|p=18-A}} The ''[[Honolulu Star-Bulletin]]'' wrote that "the lyrics are too much" and that "after a few listenings, the subject matter becomes clear, and the message gets across." The review pointed out that "musically, the song is as fine as it is lyrically inventive" and that it "grips with heretofore taboo themes."{{sfn|Donnelly, Dave|1967|p=H-5}}

In 2024, ''[[Rolling Stone]]'' ranked the song at #51 on its 200 Greatest Country Songs of All Time ranking.<ref>{{Cite magazine|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/best-country-songs-1234986540/bobbie-gentry-ode-to-billie-joe-5-1235013289/|title =The 200 Greatest Country Songs of All Time|magazine = [[Rolling Stone]]|date =May 24, 2014}}</ref>

